{"reference":"Daniel 8","book":"Daniel","chapter":8,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Reading the Vision","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Daniel 8 itself identifies the ram as Media-Persia and the goat as Greece. The later horn, whose actions include attacking the sanctuary, aligns most closely with Antiochus IV’s persecution; this does not justify mapping the vision with certainty onto present-day rulers, peoples, or states, or flattening Daniel’s little-horn imagery into a single timeless profile. Daniel 8 also begins the book’s return to Hebrew after the Aramaic section that began at 2:4b. That language pattern is important for reading the book’s structure, but it does not by itself establish when each section was written or prove a particular theory of composition.","verse_refs":["Daniel 8:1-27","Daniel 8:8-27"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_daniel_008_4e4cd820f022357fc915d54c"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}